t work stream, separate `docs-authoring` epic. |\n\n### Epic boundary analysis (E001)\n\nHLR-1 and HLR-2 are kept in a single epic: same component (Dashboard), same\nteam (RHOAI Dashboard), and the strategy frames them as one logical change —\n\"a single report in the RHOAI web console showing idle and stale resources per\nproject\". Both report sections share the same per-project report surface, and\nHLR-3's CSV export spans both; splitting would produce two epics building\nparts of the same UI surface plus an artificial serialization edge.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Affected Components owner-team table, no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then), and no Non-Functional Requirements. Team assignments were taken from `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/` instead. Non-blocking; human verification recommended.\n- **Passive-component assumption (scope trap)**: the strategy asserts the notebook controller's last-activity data \"exists\" and only needs surfacing. Architecture context (`notebooks.md`) confirms activity is tracked via Jupyter server API probes, but does not specify that it is exposed in a form the Dashboard can read through the Kubernetes API. If last-activity is not readable cluster-side, the Notebooks component (Workbenches team) would need a code change to expose it — that would add an epic and a Notebooks → Dashboard dependency edge. Treated as an implementation-detail/API-surface-discovery item (AC on E001) per the strategy's explicit \"Open Questions: None\"; flagging here for human verification.\n- **Documentation epic team**: no team data exists for the `Documentation` component (no architecture-context file). Assigned to RHOAI Dashboard by default; reassign if a docs team owns this content.\n- **Re-split trigger for E001**: E001 covers two report integrations (notebook-controller activity data and KFP run metadata) plus CSV export in o